summary:
Bridging the gap between saturated benchmarks and real-world use ("Intern LMs" by
@karpathy) is hard as evals that can be done automatically are already, and will keep getting optimized.
Thus designing new evals that push the frontier is perpetually challenging and impactful.

The effects of what we can grade easily shaping education, recruitment, and the skills we incentivize is so underrated. Builds a major gap between what works and is useful vs what we learn. This is why projects and internships &gt;&gt;&gt; courses.
Quoting @adhirajhangal
@karpathy The math/theory vs physical implementation dichotomy mirrors Turing vs von Neumann approaches to computing. Universities push abstract math because it grades easily while industry rewards hardware mastery that ships products.

New research: are prompting and activation steering just two sides of the same coin?
@EricBigelow @danielwurgaft @EkdeepL and coauthors argue they are: ICL and steering have formally equivalent effects. (1/4)
